"00_rect_protocol.mst — Dolphin's rectangle protocol on the Win32 RECT
 (DolphinDart DD10).

 `st/prims/structs/RECT.mst` is GENERATED from winkb: four fields at four
 offsets, and nothing else — which is all a struct definition is. Dolphin
 treats the same struct as a geometric object and sends it Smalltalk
 rectangle protocol; its own `UI.CreateWindowApiCall` writes

     rectangle := RECT origin: positionPoint extent: extentPoint

 and `UI.CreateWindow>>create:` then reads `rectangle left/top/width/height`.

 So this is the PROTOCOL half of a class whose LAYOUT is generated. It lives
 here rather than in the generator because the generator's input is Win32
 metadata, which has no opinion about Smalltalk protocol — and hand-editing
 the generated file is forbidden.

 LATE LAYER, necessarily: `st/prims/structs` loads after `st/dolphin_compat`,
 so a reopen there would be overwritten by the generated class.

 Both RECT and RECTL get it. They are the same shape — four 32-bit edges —
 and the corpus uses whichever the API it is calling declares."
ExternalMemory subclass: RECT [
    "Dolphin's constructor: two Points, stored as edges."
    RECT class >> origin: originPoint extent: extentPoint [
        | r |
        r := self new.
        r left: originPoint x.
        r top: originPoint y.
        r right: originPoint x + extentPoint x.
        r bottom: originPoint y + extentPoint y.
        ^r
    ]
    RECT class >> origin: originPoint corner: cornerPoint [
        | r |
        r := self new.
        r left: originPoint x. r top: originPoint y.
        r right: cornerPoint x. r bottom: cornerPoint y.
        ^r
    ]
    width [ ^self right - self left ]
    height [ ^self bottom - self top ]
    origin [ ^self left @ self top ]
    corner [ ^self right @ self bottom ]
    extent [ ^self width @ self height ]
    "A Smalltalk Rectangle from the struct, for code that wants to compute
     with it rather than pass it to Windows."
    asRectangle [ ^Rectangle origin: self origin corner: self corner ]

    "See the note on `RECTL class >> marshal:`. Both shapes get it, for the
     same reason both get the rest of this protocol: the corpus uses whichever
     one the API it is calling declares."
    RECT class >> marshal: aRectangle [
        self == aRectangle class ifTrue: [ ^aRectangle ].
        ^self origin: aRectangle origin corner: aRectangle corner
    ]
]

ExternalMemory subclass: RECTL [
    RECTL class >> origin: originPoint extent: extentPoint [
        | r |
        r := self new.
        r left: originPoint x.
        r top: originPoint y.
        r right: originPoint x + extentPoint x.
        r bottom: originPoint y + extentPoint y.
        ^r
    ]
    RECTL class >> origin: originPoint corner: cornerPoint [
        | r |
        r := self new.
        r left: originPoint x. r top: originPoint y.
        r right: cornerPoint x. r bottom: cornerPoint y.
        ^r
    ]
    width [ ^self right - self left ]
    height [ ^self bottom - self top ]
    origin [ ^self left @ self top ]
    corner [ ^self right @ self bottom ]
    extent [ ^self width @ self height ]
    asRectangle [ ^Rectangle origin: self origin corner: self corner ]

    "Dolphin's own coercion, verbatim in behaviour (`External.RECT class >>
     marshal:`): answer the argument AS an instance of the receiver, and pass
     it straight back when it is already one. `View>>rectangle:` is
     `rect := RECTL marshal: aRectangle`, so every geometry setter in the view
     layer arrives through here — including the ones BorderLayout drives."
    RECTL class >> marshal: aRectangle [
        self == aRectangle class ifTrue: [ ^aRectangle ].
        ^self origin: aRectangle origin corner: aRectangle corner
    ]
]

"Dolphin sends `POINTL marshal: aPoint` from the coordinate-mapping methods
 (`mapPoint:`, `screenToClient:` and kin) for the same reason `RECTL` needs
 it — a Smalltalk Point has to become eight bytes before Windows sees it."
ExternalMemory subclass: POINTL [
    POINTL class >> x: anX y: aY [
        | p |
        p := self new.
        p x: anX.
        p y: aY.
        ^p
    ]
    POINTL class >> marshal: aPoint [
        self == aPoint class ifTrue: [ ^aPoint ].
        ^self x: aPoint x y: aPoint y
    ]
    asPoint [ ^self x @ self y ]
]

ExternalMemory subclass: POINT [
    POINT class >> x: anX y: aY [
        | p |
        p := self new.
        p x: anX.
        p y: aY.
        ^p
    ]
    POINT class >> marshal: aPoint [
        self == aPoint class ifTrue: [ ^aPoint ].
        ^self x: aPoint x y: aPoint y
    ]
    asPoint [ ^self x @ self y ]
]

"── `RECTL>>scaleToDpi:from:` — from OS.RECTL.cls, which winkb cannot give us ──
 Our RECTL is GENERATED from winkb (layout, offsets, typed accessors), so it
 carries none of the Smalltalk Dolphin declares on `OS.RECTL.cls`. This method
 is one of those, and it is on the critical path of loading any view resource:

     View>>createWindow:      aCreateWindow scaleToDpiOf: creationParent
     CreateWindowFunction     aView scaleChildCreation: self
     View>>scaleChildCreation: aCreateWindow scaleToDpi: self dpi
     CreateWindowApiCall      rectangle scaleToDpi: anInteger from: dpi   <- here

 A saved resource records its geometry at the DPI it was designed at (96), and
 this rescales it in place to the monitor's. Transcribed from the corpus,
 including the unrolled form and the `* desired // current` order — integer
 multiply BEFORE divide, so the rounding matches Dolphin's."
RECTL extend [
    scaleToDpi: desiredInteger from: currentInteger [
        self int32AtOffset: 0
             put: (self int32AtOffset: 0) * desiredInteger // currentInteger.
        self int32AtOffset: 4
             put: (self int32AtOffset: 4) * desiredInteger // currentInteger.
        self int32AtOffset: 8
             put: (self int32AtOffset: 8) * desiredInteger // currentInteger.
        self int32AtOffset: 12
             put: (self int32AtOffset: 12) * desiredInteger // currentInteger.
        ^self
    ]
]
